# Pantry Scanner udev rule
#
# This rule creates a stable symlink at /dev/input/pantry-scanner and grants read
# access to GID 65532 (the distroless container's uid/gid) for the barcode scanner's
# evdev device.
#
# The symlink MUST be under /dev/input because the container bind-mounts only that
# directory (not a single node). udev writes a relative symlink, so /dev/pantry-scanner
# would not exist inside the container, and even if it did, its target would not resolve
# there either.
#
# Per-device scoping now comes from node ownership and mode (0640 GID 65532) rather than
# from the container seeing only one node. With the directory mount, the container sees
# all of /dev/input, but only the node the udev rule granted 0640 65532 is readable by
# the container process (uid 65532, GID 65532). Other input nodes stay root:input 0600.
#
# Why not add the service user to the "input" group?
# ---------------------------------------------------
# Adding the service user to the "input" group would grant read access to
# EVERY input device on the box - all keyboards, all mice, all touchpads.
# This turns Pantry into a keylogger for any keyboard ever attached.
#
# The distroless container image runs as uid 65532. The udev rule grants read
# access to GID 65532 so the container process can read from /dev/input/pantry-scanner.
#
# USB scanners:
#   Get vendor/product ID from `lsusb | grep -i scanner`
#   Use ID_VENDOR_ID and ID_MODEL_ID properties (set by input_id builtin) rather than
#   ATTRS{idVendor/idProduct}, which require walking the parent chain and are order-sensitive.
#   Example: Bus 001 Device 005: ID XXXX:YYYY Symbol Technologies, Inc. Scanner
#
# Bluetooth scanners:
#   Find the device name from `/proc/bus/input/devices` or `udevadm info`
#   Use ATTRS{name} since that attribute lives on the input device itself.
#   Example: cat /proc/bus/input/devices | grep -A5 -B5 -i scanner
#
# For either type, uncomment the appropriate rule below and replace the
# placeholder values with your scanner's actual identifiers.

# USB scanner example (uncomment and edit):
# SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="XXXX", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="YYYY", \
#   SYMLINK+="input/pantry-scanner", GROUP="65532", MODE="0640"

# Bluetooth scanner example (uncomment and edit - replace *Scanner* with actual name):
# SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ATTRS{name}=="*Scanner*", \
#   SYMLINK+="input/pantry-scanner", GROUP="65532", MODE="0640"
